<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp7sec6" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Orthogonal Projection</title>

<p>
In classical Euclidean geometry, given a line and a point off of the line, we can <em>drop the perpendicular</em> from the point to the line.  I now rephrase this in the language of linear algebra.
</p>

<image source="images/7.6_orthogonal_projection1.png" width="100%"/>

<p>
Given vectors <m>\vect{v},\vect{w}\in\R^m</m>,  we can <em>orthogonally project</em> <m>\vect{w}</m> onto the line <m>\spn(\vect{v})</m>.  
</p>



<p>
We may use some trigonometry and the Angle Equation <xref ref="eq_inner_prod_cos"/> to find the length of this projection.  
</p>

<md>
    <mrow>\text{Projection Length } \amp = \norm{\vect{w}}\cos(\theta) </mrow>
    <mrow> \amp = \frac{1}{\norm{\vect{v}}}\ip{\vect{v}}{\vect{w}}.</mrow>
</md>

<p>
This projection vector can now be computed.
</p>

<md>
    <mrow> \text{Projection Vector } \amp = \text{Length} \times \text{Unit Direction}</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp =\left(\frac{1}{\norm{\vect{v}}}\ip{\vect{v}}{\vect{w}}\right)\left(\frac{1}{\norm{\vect{v}}}\vect{v}\right)</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp = \frac{\ip{\vect{v}}{\vect{w}}}{\ip{\vect{v}}{\vect{v}}}\vect{v}</mrow>
</md>

<definition xml:id="def-projection_component">
    <title>Projection Component</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
        The <term>projection component</term> of <m>\vect{w}</m> onto <m>\vect{v}</m> is the vector:
        </p>
        <me>\mathrm{proj}_{\vect{v}}(\vect{w}) 
        %= ||\vect{w}|| \cos(\theta) \ \widehat{\vect{v}} 
        %= \ip{\vect{w}}{\widehat{\vect{v}}} \widehat{\vect{v}} 
        = \frac{\ip{\vect{v}}{\vect{w}}}{\ip{\vect{v}}{\vect{v}}}\vect{v}.</me>
    </statement>
</definition>

<p>
Observe that <m>\mathrm{proj}_{\vect{v}}(\vect{w})  \in \spn(\vect{v})</m>.  
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-orthogonal_component">
    <title>Orthogonal Component</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
        The <term>orthogonal component</term> of <m>\vect{w}</m> relative to <m>\vect{v}</m> is the vector:
        </p>
        <me>\mathrm{orth}_{\vect{v}}(\vect{w})= \vect{w} - \mathrm{proj}_{\vect{v}}(\vect{w}).</me>
    </statement>
</definition>

<p>
Observe that <m>\mathrm{orth}_{\vect{v}}(\vect{w})  \in \spn(\vect{v})^\perp</m>.  
</p>






<p>
Viewing <m>\vect{w}</m> as the hypotenuse of a right triangle, its legs become the projection component and orthogonal component.  
Every vector has such a decomposition.
</p>

    <interactive geogebra="vgtq44gs" width="90%" aspect="5:4" reset-icon="yes" shift-drag-zoom="yes">
    <instructions>
            <p>
              An interactive GeoGebra window showing projection components and orthogonal components.  Move the vector <m>\vect{w}</m> around and observe how the projection component and orthogonal component change.   
            </p>
     </instructions>
        <static>
            <image source="images/7.6_orthogonal_projection2.png" width="100%"/>
            <shortdescription>An image of a vector scaled by a factor of 2.5.</shortdescription>
        </static>
    </interactive>

<p>
Play with projections at GeoGebra ID: <url href="https://www.geogebra.org/m/vgtq44gs " visual=""><alert>vgtq44gs</alert></url>.
</p>

<p>
These ideas are at are at the core of the results in the next couple sections and are also incredible valuable in understanding vector calculus.  
</p>

<p>
Suppose now that <m>S\subset \R^m</m> is independent.
We may generalize the ideas of orthogonal projection from lines to considering orthogonal projections onto <m>W = \spn(S)</m>.
</p>

<p>
Given any <m>\vect{b}\in \R^m</m>, we can <em>drop the perpendicular</em> to a point on <m>W</m>.
This gives us a decomposition of <m>\vect{b}</m>,
</p>

<me>\vect{b} = \mathrm{proj}_W(\vect{b})+\mathrm{orth}_W(\vect{b}),</me>

<p>
where
</p>

<p>
<m>\mathrm{proj}_W(\vect{b})\in W</m>  is its <alert>projection component</alert> 
</p>

<p>
and
</p>

<p>
<m>\mathrm{orth}_W(\vect{b})\in W^\perp</m> is its <alert>orthogonal component</alert>.
</p>

<image source="images/7.6_orthogonal_projection3d.png" width="100%"/>

<p>
How do we actually find <m>\mathrm{proj}_W(\vect{b})</m>?
</p>

<p>
Since <m>\vect{b} = \mathrm{proj}_W(\vect{b})+\mathrm{orth}_W(\vect{b})</m>, 
</p>

<md>
    <mrow> A^T \vect{b} \amp =A^T(\mathrm{proj}_W(\vect{b}))+A^T(\mathrm{orth}_W(\vect{b}))</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp = A^T(\mathrm{proj}_W(\vect{b})) </mrow>
    <mrow>\amp  = (A^TA)[\mathrm{proj}_W(\vect{b})]_S.</mrow>
</md>

<proposition xml:id="prop-independent_implies_invertible_gram">
    <title>Independent <m>S</m> Implies Invertible Gram Matrix</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
        If <m>S</m> is independent, then <m>A^TA</m> is invertible.
        </p>
    </statement>
</proposition>


<proof>
    <p>
    A square matrix is invertible if and only if its null space is trivial.  
    Pick <m>\vect{x}\in \Null(A^TA)</m>.  I will show that it must be zero.  
    </p>
    <p>
    By definition of null space,  <m>(A^TA)\vect{x}=\vect{0}</m>.  By associativity of matrix multiplication, <m>0 = (A^TA)\vect{x}=A^T(A\vect{x})</m>, and by <xref ref="eq_orth_complements"/>,  <m>A\vect{x}\in \spn(S)^\perp</m>.  
    Meanwhile, the definition of matrix multiplication implies <m>A\vect{x}\in \spn(S)</m>.  That means <m>\norm{A\vect{x}}^2=\ip{A\vect{x}}{A\vect{x}}=0</m>, and hence <m>A\vect{x}=0</m>.
    So <m>A\vect{x}=\vect{0}</m> and the positive definiteness of the inner product <xref ref="prop_fundamental_standard_inner_product_props"/> implies <m>\vect{x}\in \Null(A)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
    By assumption, <m>S</m> is independent, and therefore, by <xref ref="prop-dependency_null_2"/>, <m>\Null(A)=\{\vect{0}\}</m> 
    and thus it must be the case that <m>\vect{x}=\vect{0}</m>.
    </p>
</proof>

<p>
    Since the Gram matrix is invertible, we may multiply by its inverse to solve for the <m>S</m>-coordinates of the projection component of <m>\vect{b}</m>,
</p>

<me xml:id="eq-lls_matrix">
[\mathrm{proj}_W(\vect{b})]_S =(A^TA)^{-1} A^T \vect{b},
</me>

<p>
and finally, we change back to standard coordinates,
</p>

<me xml:id="eq-projection_matrix">
\mathrm{proj}_W(\vect{b}) = A (A^TA)^{-1} A^T \vect{b}.
</me>

<definition xml:id="def-orthogonal_projection_matrix">
    <title>Orthogonal Projection Matrix</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
        If <m>A</m> is a matrix with independent columns, then <m>A (A^TA)^{-1} A^T</m> is the <term>orthogonal projection matrix</term> onto <m>\Col(A)</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>

<p>
    Multiplying by this matrix has the effect of <em>dropping the perpendicular!!!</em>
</p>

<p>
    Let <m>A=[\vect{v}_1| \cdots|\vect{v}_n]</m> be an <m>m\times n</m> matrix with independent columns, <m>W = \mathrm{Col}(A)</m>, <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots,\vect{v}_n\}</m>, and <m>\vect{b} \in \R^m</m>,
</p>

<p>
    If the columns of <m>A</m> do not span <m>\R^n</m>, the matrix equation 
</p>

<me>
    A\vect{x} = \vect{b} \hspace{2pc} \text{generically has no solution.}
</me>

<p>
    but we can find "the closest possible solution," namely the orthogonal projection onto the column space, i.e. the matrix equation
</p>

<me>
    A\vect{x} = \mathrm{proj}_W(\vect{b}) \hspace{2pc} \text{always has a solution.}
</me>

<p>
    Since <m>\mathrm{proj}_W(\vect{b})</m> is the point on <m>W</m> that is closest to <m>\vect{b}</m>, the solution to <em>this</em> matrix equation is the best possible <em>approximate</em> solution to the original matrix equation.
    Since distance is given by a sum of squares, this solution minimizes the sum of squares.  
    As such we call this solution the <em>linear least squares (LLS) solution</em>.  
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-lls_solution">
    <title>Linear Least Squares (LLS) Solution</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
        If <m>A</m> is an <m>m\times n</m> matrix with independent columns, <m>S</m>, and <m>\vect{b}\in \R^m</m>, then the <term>linear least squares solution</term> to the matrix equation <m>A\vect{x}=\vect{b}</m> is
        </p>
        <me>\vect{x}_{LLS} = [\mathrm{proj}_{\Col(A)}(\vect{b})]_S =(A^TA)^{-1} A^T \vect{b}.</me>
    </statement>
</definition>

<p>
    Unfortunately in the wild, the LLS solution is often denoted with a hat, <m>\widehat{\vect{x}}</m>, however this could be confused with normalized vectors.  To avoid confusion, I will denote it denoted <m>\vect{x}_{LLS}</m>.
</p>

<p>
    LLS solutions have many important applications.  For example, in statistics, when we have data sets and want to find a predictive curve (e.g. line) of ``best fit.''
</p>

<example>
    <statement>
        <p>
        We  measure a quantity we believe varies linearly over time.  
        We measure: <m>y=2.1</m> at <m>x=0</m>, <m>y=3.5</m> at <m>x=1</m>, and <m>y=3.9</m> at <m>x=3</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
        We want to find the best possible <m>f(x) = a_0+a_1x</m>. 
        There is noise, so it will not be a perfect fit. 
        </p>
        <p>
        If the data was perfectly linear, it would satisfy:
        </p>
        <md>
            <mrow> f(0) = a_0 1 + a_10</mrow>
            <mrow> f(1) = a_0 1 + a_11</mrow>
            <mrow> f(2) = a_0 1 + a_12</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>
        This amounts to solving the following matrix equation:
        </p>
        <me>
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1\amp 0\\
        1\amp 1\\
        1\amp 2\\
        \end{bmatrix}
        \begin{bmatrix}a_0\\a_1\end{bmatrix}=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        2.1\\
        3.5\\
        3.9\\
        \end{bmatrix}
        </me>
        <p>
        The data is not perfectly linear, and correspondingly, there is no solution to this matrix equation, but there is a LLS solution!!! 
        </p>
        <p>
        Check for yourself that <m>f(x)\approx 2.27+0.9x</m> is an excellent fit for this data.  
        </p>
    </statement>
</example>

<p>
    Having gone through this example, let's walk through the general case. out in the wild, there are two covarying quantities, <m>x</m> an independent quantity and <m>y</m> a dependent quantity.  
    You suspect that the underlying relationship between <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> can be modeled as a linear combination of linearly independent functions (e.g. monomials, trigonometric functions, exponentials, etc.)  
    <m>y= f(x) = a_0 f_1(x) + \cdots a_rf_r(x)</m>.
    To make this concrete, I will explicitly walk through linear relationships.
    So you suspect <m>f(x) = a_01 + a_1x</m> for some unknown <m>a_0</m> and <m>a_1</m>.
    You go out and make <m>m</m> measurements at regular intervals of <m>x</m>, let's say at times <m>x=0,\ldots, m-1</m>.  
    Let <m>\vect{y}</m> denote the column vector of data (a.k.a. observables).
</p>

<p>
If the data was perfectly linear, it would satisfy:
</p>

<md>
    <mrow> f(0) \amp = a_0 1 + a_10</mrow>
    <mrow> f(1) \amp = a_0 1 + a_11</mrow>
    <mrow> f(2) \amp = a_0 1 + a_12</mrow>
    <mrow>\vdots\hspace{1pc}\amp \hspace{1pc}\vdots</mrow>
    <mrow>f(m-1) \amp = a_0 1 + a_1(m-1)</mrow>
</md>

<p>
This amounts to solving the following matrix equation:
</p>

<me>
\begin{bmatrix}
1\amp 0\\
1\amp 1\\
1\amp 2\\
\vdots\amp \vdots\\
1\amp m-1\\
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}a_0\\a_1\end{bmatrix}
= 
\vect{y}
</me>

<p>
    The data is not perfectly linear, and correspondingly, there is no solution to this matrix equation, but there is a LLS solution.
    The LLS solution for <m>\vect{a}</m> gives line of best fit for the data you observe!!!
</p>




<exercises>
    <title>Problems</title>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the triangle <m>\triangle ABC</m> depicted below.  Observe that <m>d</m> is the segment formed by dropping the perpendicular from <m>B</m> to segment <m>AC</m>, and <m>D</m> is the point of intersection.
                Let <m>\theta =\angle ACB</m> denote the angle formed at vertex <m>C</m>.
                Using the Pythagorean Theorem, carefully show that
            </p>
            <me>c^2=a^2+b^2-2ab \cos(\theta).</me>
            <p>
                This important result is called the <term>Law of Cosines</term>.
            </p>
            <figure>
                <image source="images_hw/hw13_law_of_cosines.png" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the triangle <m>\triangle PQR</m> in the plane with vertices <m>P=(4,1)</m>, <m>Q=(-1,3)</m>, <m>R=(0,0)</m>.   
            </p>
            <ol marker="a">
                <li>Use GeoGebra to draw and label this triangle.</li>
                <li>Use the GeoGebra length and angle tools to measure the 3 sides and the 3 angles.</li>
                <li>Let <m>\vect{v}=\begin{bmatrix}4\\1\end{bmatrix}</m> and let <m>\vect{w} = \left[\begin{array}{r} -1\\3\end{array}\right]</m>. Use these vectors to find a single <m>3\times 3</m> inner product matrix who entries encode all of the geometric data for this triangle.</li>
                <li>Use the entries of this inner product matrix algebraically find the side lengths <m>PQ</m>, <m>QR</m>, and <m>PR</m>. Give <em>both</em> exact and numerically approximated solutions. Make sure they agree with the GeoGebra measurements.</li>
                <li>Verify that the side lengths satisfy the <term>Triangle Inequality</term>: any side length is less than the sum of the other two side lengths.</li>
                <li>Use the entries of this inner product matrix algebraically find the angle measures <m>\angle PRQ</m> and <m>\angle PQR</m>. Give <em>both</em> exact and numerically approximated solutions. Make sure they agree with the GeoGebra measurements.</li>
                <li>Why is <m>\angle RPQ</m> not immediately found from the inner product matrix? What information about triangles can you use to then find it, given your knowledge of the other two angles?</li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                (a,b,d,f) The GeoGebra image should look like:
            </p>
            <figure>
                <image source="images_hw/sol_hw12_02.png" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                (c) Let <m>A=[\vect{u}|\vect{v}|\vect{w}] = \left[\begin{array}{rrr}4\amp -1\amp -5\\1\amp 3\amp 2\end{array}\right]</m>.  
                Then the inner product matrix 
                <m>A^TA = \left[\begin{array}{rrr}17\amp -1\amp -18\\-1\amp 10\amp 11\\-18\amp 11\amp 29\end{array}\right]</m> contains the geometric data of this triangle.
            </p>
            <p>
                (d) Lengths can be found by computing square roots of the diagonal entries, <m>\sqrt{17}\approx4.12, \sqrt{10}\approx 3.16, \sqrt{29}\approx 5.23</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                (e) This can be done with direct checks, for example <m>5.23 \lt 4.12+ 3.16</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                (f) Use the <term>Angle Formula</term> for the off diagonal entries.
            </p>
            <me>
                \arccos\left(\frac{-1}{\sqrt{17}\sqrt{10}}\right) \approx 94.4^\circ
                \hspace{2pc}
                \arccos\left(\frac{11}{\sqrt{10}\sqrt{29}}\right) \approx 49.76^\circ
            </me>
            <p>
                (g) Look at the picture, <m>\angle RPQ</m> is not realized as an angle between any of these vectors, but rather, its supplement is realized between <m>\vect{u}</m> and <m>\vect{w}</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Rigorously prove the <xref ref="prop-orth_implies_indep"/>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Rigorously prove the <xref ref="prop-orth_complement_subspace"/>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>\vect{v} = \left[ \begin{array}{r}1\\1\\1 \end{array}\right]</m>.
            </p>
            <ol marker="a">
                <li>Use matrix transpose and your knowledge of matrix equations to find the orthogonal plane <m>W = \vect{v}^\perp</m> to <m>\vect{v}</m>. Express this in parametric vector form. Set up and explain all steps, though you may use GeoGebra for row reduction.</li>
                <li>Use GeoGebra to plot the vector <m>\vect{v}</m>, the line <m>L = \spn(\vect{v})</m>, and the plane <m>W</m>. Using the angle measurement tool, verify that the two basis vectors you found for <m>W</m> form right angle with <m>\vect{v}</m>.</li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                (a) Using our knowledge of inner products as matrix multiplication by the transpose:
                <m>V = \mathrm{Null}(\vect{v}^T)= \mathrm{Null}(\begin{bmatrix}1\amp 1\amp 1\end{bmatrix})</m>, so <m>x</m> is determined and <m>y</m> and <m>z</m> are free, hence:
            </p>
            <md>
                <mrow>x \amp = -1y - 1z</mrow>
                <mrow>y \amp = 1y + 0z</mrow>
                <mrow>z \amp = 0y + 1z</mrow>
            </md>
            <p>
                Hence 
            </p>
            <me>
                V = \left\{
                s\left[ \begin{array}{r} -1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{array} \right]
                +
                t\left[ \begin{array}{r} -1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{array} \right]
                \ \middle| \ s,t\in\R\right\}.
            </me>
            <p>
                (b) The GeoGebra image should look like:
            </p>
            <figure>
                <image source="images_hw/sol_hw13_01.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>S = \{\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_2\} = \left\{
                \left[ \begin{array}{r}3\\4\\-1 \end{array} \right],
                \left[ \begin{array}{r}2\\3\\2\end{array} \right]
                \right\}\subset \R^3</m> and consider the plane <m>W=\spn(S)</m>.
            </p>
            <ol marker="a">
                <li>Use matrix transpose and your knowledge of matrix equations to find the orthogonal line <m>L</m> to <m>W</m>. Express <m>L</m> in parametric vector form. Set up and explain all steps, though you may use GeoGebra for row reduction.</li>
                <li>Use the cross product formula to find the orthogonal line. Verify that this is the same line as in the previous part.</li>
                <li>Use GeoGebra to plot the plane <m>W</m> and the line <m>L</m>. Using the angle measurement tool, verify that the vector you found forms a right angle with both vectors in <m>S</m>.</li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                (a) Let <m>A=\left[ \begin{array}{rr}3\amp 2\\4\amp 3\\-1\amp 2\end{array}\right]</m>. Using our knowledge of inner products as matrix multiplication by the transpose, the orthogonal line is:
                <m>L = \mathrm{Null}(A^T)</m>.  
                Since
            </p>
            <me>
                \left[ \begin{array}{rrr|c}3\amp 4\amp -1\amp 0\\2\amp 3\amp 2\amp 0\end{array}\right]
                \sim
                \left[ \begin{array}{rrr|c}1\amp 0\amp -11\amp 0\\0\amp 1\amp 8\amp 0\end{array}\right]
            </me>
            <p>
                we see <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are determined and <m>z</m> is free, hence:
            </p>
            <md>
                <mrow>x \amp = 11z</mrow>
                <mrow>y \amp = -8z</mrow>
                <mrow>z \amp = 1z</mrow>
            </md>
            <p>
                Hence 
            </p>
            <me>
                L = \left\{
                s\left[ \begin{array}{r}11\\-8\\1\end{array}\right]
                \ \middle| \ s\in\R\right\}.
            </me>
            <p>
                (b) The GeoGebra image should look like:
            </p>
            <figure>
                <image source="images_hw/sol_hw13_02.png" width="60%"/>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
</exercises>


</section>